tools/CLAUDE.md:782:| Codex GPT-6 Astra (xhigh) -- *** MIGRATED FROM 5.6-SOL BY OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-09-04 ("GPT 6 Astra came out; use extra high effort"), VERIFIED LIVE BEFORE THE EDIT: `-m gpt-6-astra -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh"` returned the liveness token, named itself `GPT-6`, and quoted the first line of CHECKPOINT.md verbatim.  THE MODEL WAS REACHABLE ONLY AFTER A CLI UPGRADE THAT THE CLI COULD NOT DO ITSELF: 0.148.0 answered `The 'gpt-6-astra' model requires a newer version of Codex`, `codex update` failed twice with `Downloaded Codex package archive did not contain the expected package layout` (its staging dir was EMPTY -- the extraction, not the archive: the 0.153.4 tar.gz fetched from GitHub by hand had exactly the 0.148.0 layout), so the archive was unpacked into `~/.codex/packages/standalone/releases/0.153.4-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc/` and `current` re-pointed by junction, with 0.148.0 kept as `current.0.148.0-bak` for a one-command rollback.  `codex --version` now says 0.153.4.  EFFORT RAISED TO `max` BY OWNER DIRECTIVE THE SAME EVENING: accepted by the CLI (header `reasoning effort: max`, token returned) and PROVEN VALIDATED by the grok discriminator -- a bogus value errors with `Supported values are: 'none', 'minimal', 'low', 'med...`, so acceptance proves the tier; `panel.sh` carried `CODEX_EFFORT="max"` from that evening until 2026-09-26.  *** RAISED AGAIN TO `ultra` BY OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-09-26 ("Astra (ultra)"), AND THIS ROW SAID `max` UNTIL 2026-10-02.  `panel.sh`'s own comment beside the variable records the measurement: the SERVER's enum stops at `max` and rejects a bogus value (400 invalid_enum_value), while `ultra` is ACCEPTED by the CLI (rc 0, header `reasoning effort: ultra`, liveness token plus a verbatim CHECKPOINT.md quote) -- the CLI's model cache calls it "Maximum reasoning with automatic task delegation", a CLI tier above max.  **DO NOT READ THE EFFORT OUT OF THIS ROW; read `grep -n '^CODEX_EFFORT=' GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh`.**  OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-10-01 ("Skip any 5.6 SOL just use Astra if using Codex; if Astra AUP fails gpt-daybreak-blue-latest"): the codex seat is GPT-6 Astra ONLY -- never 5.6-SOL -- and `gpt-daybreak-blue-latest` is fired only when Astra returns a content-filter (AUP) refusal; `tools/cxlaunch.sh` re-runs a refused brief once on it. ***  Re-check the model name after any further CLI upgrade -- a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE. *** | `codex exec --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check -m gpt-6-astra -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh" "$(cat brief)" < /dev/null` (the 5.6-SOL form until 2026-09-04) — the `< /dev/null` is required. Windows argv caps ~32 KB. *** `panel.sh` NOW HANDLES THIS ITSELF, as of 2026-08-30 -- do NOT hand-trim a brief for it. *** Above `ARGV_CAP` (30000) codex and kimi are fed a POINTER (`PTR_PROMPT`: read `$BRIEF_ABS` and review it as its own Questions section directs) instead of the contents; below it the inline form is byte-unchanged. Both are FILE-READING seats, so this costs nothing -- and grok (`--prompt-file`) and agy (`--add-dir`) were already immune, leaving these two as the only argv-fed seats. **The block previously WARNED and then launched anyway**, which cost both seats on a 38 KB brief and is the check-that-cannot-fail family's sibling: *a message reporting an ACTION the code does not take*, exactly like the re-firing message. `PANEL_ARGV_CAP` overrides the cap **only so the branch is testable in both directions** without keeping a 38 KB fixture; it is not a tuning knob. Controlled by extracting the real decision block out of `panel.sh` and `eval`ing it -- 6/6 rc=0 fixed, EXTRACT FAILED rc=9 pre-fix. **A HAND-WRITTEN BRIEF STILL NEEDS TO BE READABLE**: the pointer form only helps seats that can open the file, so it is not licence to grow briefs without limit -- the packet-fed Ollama seats still get the whole thing POSTed and still burn budget on it. Never the trailing-`-` stdin form (died silently once). |
tools/CLAUDE.md:783:| agy 3.8 (high) | `agy --dangerously-skip-permissions --add-dir <dir> --print-timeout 30m --model gemini-3.8-flash-high --effort high --print "<short prompt pointing at brief file>"` — reads files via `--add-dir`; no stdin prompt mode. **Model set by user directive 2026-08-13.** PASS BOTH `--model` and `--effort`: the effort tier is baked into the model name (`-high`/`-medium`/`-low`) AND there is a separate `--effort` flag, so naming only one leaves the other at its default. Before this, `panel.sh` passed NEITHER while printing "agy(high)" — the banner was an overclaim. **`agy models` is the authority on valid names** (it lists gemini-3.7/3.6/3.5-flash and 3.1-pro tiers, plus claude-sonnet-4-6 and claude-opus-4-6-thinking); a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE, so re-check after any CLI upgrade.  *** AND IT HAS A QUOTA.  MEASURED 2026-08-30, THE FIRST agy WALL ON RECORD, AND IT ARRIVED WHILE THE SEAT WAS BEING DELIBERATELY PUSHED. ***  `Error: Individual quota reached. Please upgrade your subscription to increase your limits. Resets in 1h56m37s.` -- **111 bytes, on stderr, with the process exiting immediately**, so the `.out` file is ZERO BYTES and nothing is written to the panel dir but that one line.  **THE ROSTER ASSUMPTION IT OVERTURNS:** the owner's own read was *"seems like there is limitless agy use"*, and this project had never seen it wall, so it was being farmed 3 jobs at a time.  Six substantial invocations inside about forty minutes reached the limit.  Treat it like Grok's paid cap: **not queryable, not unlimited, and a raised cap has the same failure mode further out.**  *** THE DETECTION IS EASY AND THE MISREADING IS NOT. ***  It states its own reset time, which no other seat here does -- so a walled agy tells you exactly when to re-fire, and the re-fire-a-walled-seat-once rule becomes a scheduled thing rather than a guess.  But **the zero-byte `.out` is the trap**: this file's own rule says a zero-byte agy log is the NORMAL mid-run state because agy does not stream.  Zero bytes plus a live process is mid-run; zero bytes plus 111 bytes on stderr is a WALL.  **Check the `.err`, not the size** -- `panel.sh` writes one per seat and this is what it is for.  *Recorded because a seat that walls with the machine otherwise healthy is the state this project most often misfiles: three jobs failed identically and instantly, which reads like a launcher bug and is not.* |
tools/CLAUDE.md:784:| **Gemini web** (`gemini.google.com`; model picker **Pro** when offered, else Flash; **bank the label the app shows AFTER the reply**, because the app drops to a smaller model at capacity without refusing) | **PACKET-FED, owner-signed-in browser, one message = one packet.** Transports, in order of preference: (1) Fable in the desktop app or with the Chrome extension: the Claude in Chrome / built-in browser tools, a new tab on `gemini.google.com`, paste the packet, wait for the reply, copy it verbatim. (2) Codex: a browser MCP attached to the owner's already-signed-in Chrome: `codex mcp add chrome-devtools -- npx chrome-devtools-mcp@latest --autoConnect` (enable remote debugging at `chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging` first), or Playwright MCP with the Playwright MCP Bridge extension (`--extension`). **Never sign in from an automated fresh profile: Google refuses it (*"This browser or app may not be secure"*).** (3) Always available, and the seat until (1) or (2) has passed the control: the coordinator writes `gemini_web.packet.md` into the job directory and the owner pastes it; the reply is banked by hand. **Role:** Gemini-family reading when Agy's Gemini pool is walled; second-lens Gemini reading on packet-shaped work. **Not a file-reading seat. Not a second independent seat beside Agy-on-Gemini without the same-model disclosure** (two Gemini readings certify as disclosed same-model replication, never as two-model). |
