tools/CLAUDE.md:4063:> the note is printed in the row line, so a records-only correction shows up as an apparent

 succeeded in 246ms:
"""THE ONE PLACE A LEDGER STAGE TOKEN IS SPELLED OR CLASSIFIED.  #534, 2026-09-18.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  Four tools read ledger entries and each carried its own copy of the tokens:
check_stage_panels.py knew four, check_fable_queue.py was never taught RE_READ, gen_dashboard.py
filtered HELD markers it had no business filtering, and check_fix_seats.py knew only HELD -- so it
credited a stage-exemption entry written under `seat: fable5' as a FLAGSHIP ANSWER, and the row that
introduced the newest token passed its own flagship mandate on that token (#532 pass 2, Opus seat,
MEASURED).  One cause every time: a token added where it was needed and never mirrored.  So the
literals AND the classification live here, and precommit_check.sh refuses a token literal anywhere
else in tools/pipeline/.  *** THREE of those four readers ship with #534 and are covered by its controls;
check_fix_seats.py is another round's untracked, unwired draft -- it imports is_answer from here but NO
control covers it until it ships (fable5, #534 last seat: this sentence overclaimed until then). ***

AN ENTRY HAS TWO INDEPENDENT PROPERTIES, and the #532 code conflated them:

  is_answer(e)     -- does this entry count as the seat answering?
  exempts_stage(e) -- does this entry exempt its WHOLE (row, phase) stage from the panel rule?

                    is_answer   exempts_stage
    HELD             no           no          (a promise; handled by its own rules)
    ANNOTATION       no           yes         (pre-2026-08-16 policy)
    RE_READ          YES          yes         (a real single-seat re-read -- fable measured one
                                               rendering as agreement pre-#532, rightly)
    NOPANEL          no           yes         (no panel was convened on this stage)
    NOTSEAT          no           NO          (THIS ENTRY is a main-loop record, not a seat
                                               answer; it can never silence other entries)
    anything else    yes          no

NOTSEAT IS THE ENTRY-SCOPED MARKER, and it is the one to reach for by default.  #532 used the
stage-scoped NOPANEL for six records that meant only "this entry is not a seat answer"; on those
rows any panel LATER recorded under the same phase would have been silently exempted in advance
(fable5, #532 pass 2, R2).  A stage exemption is a claim about everything in the stage and must be
earned; see stage_token_problems() for the guard.
"""
import re

HELD = "[HELD AWAITING SEAT:"
ANNOTATION = "[RUN UNDER THE PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY]"
RE_READ = "[NOT A PANEL STAGE: SINGLE-SEAT RE-READ]"
NOPANEL = "[NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]"
NOTSEAT = "[NOT A SEAT ANSWER]"

STAGE_EXEMPT = (ANNOTATION, RE_READ, NOPANEL)
ALL = (HELD, ANNOTATION, RE_READ, NOPANEL, NOTSEAT)

PANEL_DIR = re.compile(r"panel_\d{8}_\d{6}")


def _note(e):
    return e.get("note") or ""


def is_held(e):
    return HELD in _note(e)


def exempts_stage(e):
    n = _note(e)
    #  HELD and NOTSEAT both dominate: a hold is a promise and NOTSEAT says "this entry only" -- an
    #  entry carrying either can never exempt the stage, whatever else it quotes (minimax, #534 pass 2:
    #  the docstring promised this of NOTSEAT and the code did not deliver it).
    return (not is_held(e)) and NOTSEAT not in n and any(t in n for t in STAGE_EXEMPT)


def is_answer(e):
    n = _note(e)
    if HELD in n or NOTSEAT in n or ANNOTATION in n or NOPANEL in n:
        return False
    return True          # RE_READ entries ARE answers; so is every untokened entry


HELD_SEATS = re.compile(re.escape(HELD) + r"[ \t]*([a-z0-9_]+(?:[ \t]*,[ \t]*[a-z0-9_]+)*)[ \t]*\]")
#  case-SENSITIVE, like is_held() -- they disagreed until the #534 F2 pass (codex); the ledger holds
#  zero non-canonical spellings, so this changes no real reading


MALFORMED = "?malformed-hold"
POLICY_CUTOFF = "2026-08-16"   # full panel on every stage; work before it is not judged by it


def held_seats(e):
    """The seats a HELD entry names, in order, or [] if it names none in the strict shape."""
    m = HELD_SEATS.search(_note(e))
    return [x.strip().lower() for x in m.group(1).split(",")] if m else []


def live_holds(entries):
    """Seats still awaited in one (row, phase): named by a HELD entry and NOT answered by that seat
    in this stage at a STRICTLY LATER date -- the carrier's discharge rule, applied here so every
    reader agrees on it.  (#534 pass 2, codex + Opus: two of the three stages that showed a full count
    over a hold were DISCHARGED holds, and one was live; a reader must tell them apart.)"""
    out = []
    for h in entries:
        if not is_held(h):
            continue
        hd = h.get("date") or ""
        named = held_seats(h)
        if not named:
            #  a hold in no parseable shape can never be discharged by an answer -- it holds until
            #  someone writes it properly; a malformed marker must not read as "nothing owed"
            #  (codex, #534 F2 pass: `[HELD AWAITING SEAT: glm,]` let a closed row pass H and I)
            if MALFORMED not in out:
                out.append(MALFORMED)
            continue
        for seat in named:
            done = any((e.get("seat") or "").lower() == seat and is_answer(e)
                       and (e.get("date") or "") > hd for e in entries)
            if not done and seat not in out:
                out.append(seat)
    return out


def stage_token_problems(entries):
    """Why a stage's exemption must be REFUSED, or [] if it stands.  `entries' = one (row, phase).

    (i)  STRICTLY LATER -- EVERY exemption token: a tallied answer dated after the newest exemption
         entry.  An exemption is a claim about the stage AS IT STOOD; material recorded afterwards is
         new, and nothing about a token's vintage makes it cover the future (#534 pass 2: codex, Opus,
         deepseek, minimax, kimi all refused the NOPANEL-only scope; Opus measured that clause (i) was
         never the part that refused the legacy stages).
    (ii) UNCITED PANEL -- NOPANEL ONLY: a tallied answer cites a panel dir that no exemption entry
         names.  This clause IS the retroactive one: annotations and re-reads were written before an
         exemption had to name its panels, and applying it to them refused seven legacy stages for a
         requirement nobody owed at the time.  (A stage whose answers cite no panel dir passes it --
         Opus fixture G, a DISCLOSED escape.)

    Brief-TITLE matching is deliberately absent: a correlate that missed BOTH real disputed cases
    (fable5, #532 pass 2, R4)."""
    tok = [e for e in entries if exempts_stage(e)]
    if not tok:
        return []
    newest_tok = max((e.get("date") or "") for e in tok)
    nop = [e for e in tok if NOPANEL in _note(e)]
    cited = set()
    for e in nop:
        cited |= set(PANEL_DIR.findall(_note(e) + " " + (e.get("ev") or "")))
    out = []
    for e in entries:
        if not is_answer(e) or e in tok:
            continue
        d = e.get("date") or ""
        #  a later answer that itself PREDATES the full-panel directive is not judged by it: the
        #  pre-policy stage it joined was grandfathered, and stays so (kimi, #534 F2 pass)
        if d > newest_tok and d >= POLICY_CUTOFF:
            out.append("answer by %s dated %s is LATER than the exemption (%s)"
                       % (e.get("seat"), d, newest_tok))
        if nop:
            for p in PANEL_DIR.findall(e.get("ev") or ""):
                if p not in cited:
                    out.append("answer by %s cites %s, which the exemption does not name"
                               % (e.get("seat"), p))
    return out


_CORES = {HELD: ("HELD AWAITING SEAT",), ANNOTATION: ("PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY", "RUN UNDER THE PRE"),
          RE_READ: ("SINGLE-SEAT RE-READ",), NOPANEL: ("NO PANEL CONVENED",),
          NOTSEAT: ("NOT A SEAT ANSWER",)}


def literal_offenders(paths):
    """(path, line, token) for every CODE string constant outside this module that spells a token.

    The control fable5 required with R5: the literals live here or nowhere.  Docstrings and comments
    are exempt -- they DESCRIBE tokens, and a prose mention never classified an entry.  An AST walk,
    not a grep, because a grep cannot tell a docstring from a constant and would either flag every
    history paragraph or be loosened until it flagged nothing."""
    import ast
    out = []
    for p in paths:
        src = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
        tree = ast.parse(src, p)
        docs = set()
        for n in ast.walk(tree):
            if isinstance(n, (ast.Module, ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef, ast.ClassDef)):
                b = getattr(n, "body", None)
                if b and isinstance(b[0], ast.Expr) and isinstance(b[0].value, ast.Constant):
                    docs.add(id(b[0].value))
        def fold(n):
            """a string built by explicit `+' of constants, folded -- or None"""
            if isinstance(n, ast.Constant) and isinstance(n.value, str):
                return n.value
            if isinstance(n, ast.BinOp) and isinstance(n.op, ast.Add):
                a, b = fold(n.left), fold(n.right)
                return a + b if a is not None and b is not None else None
            return None
        for n in ast.walk(tree):
            if isinstance(n, ast.BinOp) and isinstance(n.op, ast.Add):
                v = fold(n)
                hit = [t for t in ALL if v is not None and any(c in v for c in _CORES[t])]
                if hit:
                    #  #534 F2 pass (kimi, minimax, deepseek): "NOT A SEAT " + "ANSWER]" escaped the
                    #  per-constant match; fold the concatenation and test the whole
                    out.append((p, n.lineno, hit[0]))
                continue
            if isinstance(n, ast.Constant) and isinstance(n.value, str) and id(n) not in docs:
                for t in ALL:
                    #  Matched on the token's CORE PHRASE, brackets and punctuation stripped: a
                    #  fragment like "PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY" or "HELD AWAITING SEAT" classifies just
                    #  as well as the whole literal, and a whole-literal match missed it (Opus, #534
                    #  pass 2: `or "PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY" in note' passed this check).
                    if any(c in n.value for c in _CORES[t]):
                        out.append((p, n.lineno, t))
                        break
    return out


if __name__ == "__main__":
    import glob
    import os
    import sys
    if sys.argv[1:2] == ["--census"]:
        #  fable5 F2 (#534): #532 stated this population three ways ("nine", "eleven of twelve",
        #  and the true 17 stages / 23 entries) because each was counted by hand.  This is the one
        #  deriver: per token, ENTRIES and distinct (row, phase) STAGES -- two units, both named.
        import io
        import json
        led = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else os.path.join(
            os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "ledger.json")
        rows = json.load(io.open(led, encoding="utf-8"))["rows"]
        names = {HELD: "HELD", ANNOTATION: "ANNOTATION", RE_READ: "RE_READ",
                 NOPANEL: "NOPANEL", NOTSEAT: "NOTSEAT"}
        for t in ALL:
            n_ent, stages = 0, set()
            for r in rows:
                for e in r.get("entries", []):
                    if t in _note(e):
                        n_ent += 1
                        stages.add((r["id"], e.get("phase")))
            print("%-10s %4d entries  %4d stages" % (names[t], n_ent, len(stages)))
        print("STAGE_TOKEN_CENSUS  ledger=%s" % led)
        sys.exit(0)
    if sys.argv[1:2] != ["--selfcheck"]:
        print("usage: stage_tokens.py --selfcheck [files...] | --census [ledger.json]")
        sys.exit(2)
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    files = sys.argv[2:] or [f for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(here, "*.py")))
                             if os.path.basename(f) != "stage_tokens.py"]
    bad = literal_offenders(files)
    for p, ln, t in bad:
        print("  %s:%d spells %s -- import it from stage_tokens.py" % (os.path.basename(p), ln, t))
    print("STAGE_TOKENS_%s  %d file(s) scanned, %d literal(s) outside stage_tokens.py"
          % ("FAIL" if bad else "PASS", len(files), len(bad)))
    sys.exit(1 if bad else 0)
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""EVERY STAGE RUNS THE FULL PANEL BEFORE THE ROUND MOVES ON.

Owner directive, 2026-08-16, and it SUPERSEDES the carrier's older rule of "ONE full
panel per ROUND, with pass 2 restricted to codex + Opus".  That older rule was written
when Grok's free tier was dead and Kimi was quota-dead; both are healthy now, all seven
scriptable seats answered the last three panels, and the only live constraint is that the
three Ollama seats return HTTP 429 if two panels fire within ~20 minutes -- a spacing that
real stages already exceed.

THE RULE: for any stage (assess / research / review) that ran ON OR AFTER the cutoff, all
nine seats must be recorded.  If a seat cannot be run, the round STOPS and the owner is
asked -- degrading silently is exactly what this replaces.

TWO DELIBERATE EXEMPTIONS, both owner directives, both narrow:

  * `regress` is FABLE-ONLY.  Only the flagship seat reviews regression, batched.
  * stages that ran BEFORE the cutoff are GRANDFATHERED, forward-only by owner decision.
    They are not re-panelled; they carry an explicit annotation entry so the dashboard does
    not read their blanks as seat failures.  A blank cell and a seat failure must never look
    alike -- that conflation is what this whole layer exists to prevent.

Exit 0 clean, 1 on violations.  `--list` shows every stage checked.
"""
import json
import os
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
import stage_tokens as st   # #534: the ONE place a token is spelled or classified

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
#  Overridable so controls run against COPIES.  Added 2026-08-17 after a negative control
#  for the CLOSED-WHILE-HELD clause silently tested NOTHING: it wrote a mutated ledger to a
#  temp file, passed $GXLEDGER, and this script -- which had no such override -- read the real
#  ledger and reported PASS.  A VOID CONTROL AND A PASSING CONTROL ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE in
#  the output, which is the failure mode the whole harness is built against.
LEDGER = os.environ.get("GXLEDGER") or os.path.join(HERE, "ledger.json")

CUTOFF = "2026-08-16"          # stages dated on or after this must be complete

#  ---- HOLD QUESTIONS -----------------------------------------------------------------
#  A STAGE HELD *FOR* A SEAT MUST CARRY THE QUESTION IT IS HELD FOR.  That rule has been in
#  CLAUDE.md since a 46-row batch measured it: the three meta-rows carrying identical
#  boilerplate had their question RECONSTRUCTED from the row's subject, while the two that
#  named it explicitly were the easiest to answer *accurately*.
#
#  *** IT WAS AT 0 FOR 18 ON 2026-08-26. ***  Every held stage in the ledger carried a
#  DISPOSITION note where a question belongs -- "FILE - no measurable effect until a caller
#  survives", "a policy, not a round".  Each is a true statement about the row.  None is a
#  question a seat can answer.
#
#  This is the carrier diagnosing itself: the rule was PROSE in an auto-loaded file, it was
#  known, and it still did not fire.  A seat given a disposition note must GUESS what it is
#  being asked, and a guessed question yields an answer to the wrong question that still
#  LOOKS like a discharged stage -- the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape, moved one
#  step earlier.
#
#  DATED CUTOFF, owner decision 2026-08-26, and the same shape check_bugfile_sync.py uses:
#  stages whose newest entry is on or before HOLDQ_CUTOFF are REPORTED and not failed.  A
#  rule that retro-fails 18 pre-existing rows blocks the next commit on reconstruction work
#  nobody scheduled, and a rule that does that gets switched off.  New holds are enforced.
HOLDQ_CUTOFF = "2026-08-26"
FABLE_ONLY = {"regress"}       # owner directive: only the flagship reviews regression
STAGES = ("assess", "research", "review")

#  An annotation entry is not a review.  It exists to say WHY a stage is short, and it must
#  not be counted as one of the seats that answered -- otherwise marking a gap would close it.
ANNOTATION = st.ANNOTATION      # #534: spelled once, in stage_tokens.py

#  A SECOND, DIFFERENT EXEMPTION, and it is deliberately not folded into the first.
#
#  The token above asserts a specific historical fact: the stage ran before the full-panel
#  directive.  On 2026-08-21 a batched flagship pass added a single entry to a row that had been
#  FILED with one seat -- and one filing plus one re-read is counted here as a TWO-OF-NINE STAGE,
#  so section H reddened for a row nobody had convened a panel on.
#
#  *** THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT CLEARING THAT USED THE TOKEN ABOVE, WHICH WOULD HAVE PUT A FALSE
#  STATEMENT IN THE LEDGER TO TURN A GATE GREEN. ***  A 2026-08-21 re-read did not run under the
#  pre-2026-08-16 policy.  Backed out; the honest fix is a token that says what actually
#  happened, because the two situations are not the same and a reader must be able to tell them
#  apart.
#
#  This is a real recurring category rather than a one-off: any pass that sweeps filed rows can
#  promote a filing into a short stage without anyone deciding to.
RE_READ = st.RE_READ      # #534: spelled once, in stage_tokens.py

#  A THIRD EXEMPTION, 2026-09-18, and like the second it exists because neither older token was
#  TRUE of the situation -- which is the only reason this file ever gains a token.
#
#  A session read 23 UNREAD SEAT ANSWERS off disk and recorded each onto the row it discussed.
#  That is strictly an improvement to the record: this project has three times found real
#  defects sitting in an unread review.  **It took both stage gates from GREEN to RED, creating
#  19 violations where HEAD had zero** -- because recording a seat's remark about row X makes
#  row X's stage go from ABSENT (or a 1-seat filing) to a 2..8-seat "short panel", and a short
#  panel is what this check reddens on.
#
#  *** SO THE GATE FIRED BECAUSE THE RECORD GOT MORE HONEST, AND THE ONLY WAY TO GREEN IT WITH
#  THE OLDER TOKENS WOULD HAVE BEEN TO WRITE A FALSE SENTENCE INTO THE LEDGER. ***  RE_READ says
#  SINGLE-SEAT, and several of these carry 2, 3, 4, 6 and even 8 recorded seats; ANNOTATION
#  asserts a pre-2026-08-16 vintage that is simply untrue of a 2026-09-17 entry.  The comment on
#  RE_READ already records the last time someone reached for the nearest token to turn a gate
#  green, and that attempt was backed out.  This is that lesson obeyed rather than re-learned.
#
#  THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it is mechanical rather than a judgement call: read the `ev` field for
#  the panel dir, open that dir's `brief_sent.md`, and ask **WAS THIS PANEL CONVENED ON THIS
#  ROW?**  If the brief's subject is this row (or a deliberate multi-row late review naming it),
#  the stage RAN and this token is a lie.  If the brief is about some other row and a seat merely
#  mentioned this one in passing, no panel was ever convened here.
#
#  *** THAT TEST OVERTURNED THE AUTHOR'S FIRST SORTING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS WRITTEN DOWN AS A
#  COMMAND AND NOT AS ADVICE. ***  Seat COUNT was used first -- "+8 seats must mean a real panel"
#  -- and it was wrong three times over: `diskimagesign`, `pcc2c040assertrow` and
#  `pcc2genctlc040alias` all cite ONE dir, `panel_20260917_100431`, whose brief IS convened on
#  them, so they are real stages; while `asiceventaddr` carries a full EIGHT recorded seats that
#  all cite `panel_20260826_222557`, a pass 2 on a COMMENT-ONLY commit to a vendored header --
#  a different subject entirely, and therefore not a stage at all.  **The count is a CORRELATE
#  of "a panel ran"; the brief's subject is the thing itself.
#
#  *** THE TOKEN STATES THE GENERAL CLAIM AND THE NOTE CARRIES THE SPECIFIC REASON, WHICH IS A
#  DELIBERATE BREAK FROM THE TWO TOKENS ABOVE. ***  Those bake their reason into the literal,
#  and that is exactly what made RE_READ unusable here -- it says SINGLE-SEAT, so a true
#  situation with three seats had no token that fitted.  A first draft of THIS token repeated
#  the mistake: it read INCIDENTAL SEAT MENTION, which is true of eleven of the twelve stages
#  and FALSE of `ip30abortkill' assess, whose two entries are a rung-3 WITNESS BUILD and a
#  ship-order ADJUDICATION -- real work on that very row, and not a panel seat answer at all.
#  The checkable claim both gates need is only ever "no panel was convened on this stage";
#  everything else is provenance, and provenance belongs in prose a reader can check.**
NOPANEL = st.NOPANEL      # #534: spelled once, in stage_tokens.py

#  *** A STAGE DELIBERATELY HELD FOR A NAMED SEAT IS NOT A STAGE THAT MOVED ON. ***
#
#  Added 2026-08-17.  Fable usage ran high and the owner said to queue Fable work and carry on
#  with the rest.  That is the honest thing to do -- eight seats can answer now and the ninth
#  when quota returns -- but the first version of this check reported the resulting 8/9 as
#  "moved on without the full panel", which is exactly wrong: nothing moved on, the round is
#  explicitly open and waiting.  Two different states rendering the same is the defect this
#  whole layer exists to prevent, so it must not be committed BY this layer.
#
#  A HELD marker is an explicit ledger entry carrying this token and NAMING the awaited seats.
#  It is reported separately, not as a violation.
#
#  THE ANTI-LOOPHOLE, and it is the whole reason this is safe to add: a held stage on a CLOSED
#  row is a HARD violation.  You may wait for a seat; you may not close a round while waiting
#  for one.  Without that clause "HELD" would be a way to mark any gap and walk away, which is
#  worse than the conflation it replaces.
HELD = st.HELD      # #534: spelled once, in stage_tokens.py


def main(argv):
    listing = "--list" in argv
    with open(LEDGER, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        ledger = json.load(f)
    seats = [s["id"] for s in ledger["seats"]]
    n_seats = len(seats)

    #  A stage with exactly ONE seat is a FILING, not a panel that ran short.  Items enter
    #  the queue when a seat raises them during some other round's panel, which leaves a
    #  one-seat `assess` entry; the item has not started yet.  Counted and REPORTED
    #  separately rather than waved through, because "filed, awaiting its panel" and "we ran
    #  a panel and it came up short" are different states and the whole point of this layer
    #  is that different states must not render the same.
    violations, grandfathered, complete, filed, held = [], 0, 0, [], []

    for row in ledger["rows"]:
        by_stage = {}
        for ent in row.get("entries", []):
            by_stage.setdefault(ent.get("phase"), {}).setdefault("_all", []).append(ent)
            if st.exempts_stage(ent):
                by_stage[ent.get("phase")]["_annotated"] = True
            if st.is_held(ent):
                d = by_stage.setdefault(ent.get("phase"), {})
                #  A LIST, NOT A SLOT.  This assigned a single value until 2026-08-17, so a
                #  stage held for TWO seats kept only the LAST marker and reported one.
                #  Found the day it first mattered: exitsweep's assess stage is held for BOTH
                #  codex (a measured quota wall) and fable5 (deliberate conservation), and the
                #  gate printed "awaiting: fable5" -- silently dropping codex.
                #
                #  It UNDER-REPORTS THE DEBT, which is the one direction this gate must never
                #  fail in: a stage that looks like it waits for one seat gets completed a
                #  seat early, and the missing seat's blank then reads as agreement.
                d.setdefault("_held", []).append(ent.get("note", ""))
                continue
            if not st.is_answer(ent):
                continue           # ANNOTATION / NOPANEL / NOTSEAT entries are not seat answers
            d = by_stage.setdefault(ent.get("phase"), {})
            d.setdefault("seats", set()).add(ent.get("seat"))
            d["newest"] = max(d.get("newest", ""), ent.get("date", ""))

        for stage in STAGES:
            info = by_stage.get(stage)
            if not info:
                continue                       # stage never ran; not this check's business
            if "seats" not in info:
                #  #534 F2 pass (codex, Opus): a stage whose only entry is a LIVE hold was skipped
                #  here, before any hold test, so a CLOSED row could carry one with H green.
                live0 = st.live_holds(info.get("_all", []))
                if live0 and row.get("state") == "closed":
                    violations.append((row["id"], "CLOSED-WHILE-HELD", stage, 0,
                                       ["a round may not close while a stage awaits a seat"]))
                elif live0:
                    held.append((row["id"], stage, 0, ", ".join(live0), "",
                                 (row.get("hold_question") or "").strip()))
                continue
            got, newest = info["seats"], info.get("newest", "")
            #  #534 (heldmasked, an L3 of this round per its pass 2): a LIVE hold -- a named seat not
            #  answered in this stage at a strictly later date -- beats a full count.  Before this,
            #  the count test ran first, so an owner-decision hold over nine misdirected answers
            #  reported `ok 9/9` and the row could be CLOSED with H, I and the wall all green
            #  (Opus, measured).  A DISCHARGED marker does not hold a full stage.
            live = st.live_holds(info.get("_all", []))
            if len(got) >= n_seats and not live:
                complete += 1
                if listing:
                    print("  ok    %-14s %-9s %d/%d" % (row["id"], stage, len(got), n_seats))
                continue
            #  HELD is checked BEFORE the one-seat "filed" shortcut, so a stage that is
            #  genuinely waiting on a named seat is reported as waiting even if only one
            #  other seat has answered so far.
            #  ... and ONLY a live hold holds a SHORT stage either.  A marker every named seat has
            #  since answered is history: treating it as a hold printed "awaiting: fable5" over
            #  pcc2c040kill's 8/9 review whose real gap is agy (codex + Opus, #534 F2 pass).
            if live:
                if row.get("state") == "closed":
                    violations.append((row["id"], "CLOSED-WHILE-HELD", stage, len(got),
                                       ["a round may not close while a stage awaits a seat"]))
                else:
                    #  Every marker, de-duplicated, order preserved -- so two holds report
                    #  two seats and the operator sees the whole debt.
                    names = list(live)
                    held.append((row["id"], stage, len(got), ", ".join(names),
                                     newest, (row.get("hold_question") or "").strip()))
                continue
            if len(got) == 1:
                filed.append((row["id"], stage))
                continue
            if info.get("_annotated"):
                #  #534: an exemption is REFUSED when a later answer or an uncited panel shows it
                #  does not describe the stage as it now stands (stage_tokens.stage_token_problems).
                probs = st.stage_token_problems(info.get("_all", []))
                if probs:
                    violations.append((row["id"], "TOKEN-REFUSED", stage, len(got), probs))
                    continue
            if newest < CUTOFF or info.get("_annotated"):
                grandfathered += 1
                if listing:
                    print("  --    %-14s %-9s %d/%d  grandfathered"
                          % (row["id"], stage, len(got), n_seats))
                continue
            violations.append((row["id"], row.get("state", "?"), stage, len(got),
                               [s for s in seats if s not in got]))

    print("check_stage_panels: %d complete, %d grandfathered, %d filed-awaiting-panel, "
          "%d HELD, %d incomplete"
          % (complete, grandfathered, len(filed), len(held), len(violations)))
    noq_new, noq_old = [], []
    for rid, stage, n, awaited, newest, question in held:
        print("  HOLD  %-14s %-9s %d/%d  awaiting: %s" % (rid, stage, n, n_seats, awaited))
        if question:
            continue
        #  Report by DATE, not by count: "18 rows lack a question" is a backlog, while
        #  "this stage was held today and names no question" is an actionable defect.
        (noq_new if newest > HOLDQ_CUTOFF else noq_old).append((rid, stage, awaited, newest))
        print("        *** NO hold_question -- %s ***"
              % ("FAIL, held after %s" % HOLDQ_CUTOFF if newest > HOLDQ_CUTOFF
                 else "warn, held on/before %s" % HOLDQ_CUTOFF))
    if noq_old:
        print("  %d held stage(s) name no question and predate %s -- reported, not failed."
              % (len(noq_old), HOLDQ_CUTOFF))
        print("  They are real debt: a seat fired against a disposition note has to guess")
        print("  the question, and a guessed question still looks like a discharged stage.")
    if listing and filed:
        for rid, stage in filed:
            print("  ..    %-14s %-9s 1/%d  filed, not yet panelled" % (rid, stage, n_seats))
    if not violations and not noq_new:
        print("STAGE_PANELS_PASS  every post-cutoff stage ran the full panel")
        return 0

    if noq_new:
        print("STAGE_PANELS_FAIL  %d held stage(s) name no question:" % len(noq_new))
        for rid, stage, awaited, newest in noq_new:
            print("    %-14s %-9s held %s for %s -- no hold_question"
                  % (rid, stage, newest, awaited))
        print()
        print("  Put the question the seat is being asked in the row's `hold_question`")
        print("  field.  Not the disposition -- the QUESTION.  'FILE - a design change'")
        print("  tells a seat nothing it can answer.")
        if not violations:
            return 1

    print("STAGE_PANELS_FAIL  %d stage(s) moved on without the full panel:" % len(violations))
    for rid, state, stage, n, miss in violations:
        print("    %-14s %-7s %-9s %d/%d   missing: %s"
              % (rid, state, stage, n, n_seats, ", ".join(miss)))
    print()
    print("  The owner's rule is FULL PANEL ON EVERY STAGE, and STOP AND ASK if a seat")
    print("  cannot be run -- not degrade quietly.  Either fire the missing seats, or")
    print("  raise it with the owner and record the decision as an explicit entry.")
    return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))

 succeeded in 309ms:
VERDICT: 3 wrong records, 10 overtaken, 1 refuted, 1 live defects, 2 to file -- every Argon flag was already taken on the design pass or is refuted (its same-day index fix would reopen the documented 2026-08-30 same-date mis-clears); the residue is records wording (stalls and not-fired passes recorded as walls; the "V3-V6 answered" premise surviving in fableheldform's state_note) and one live instance of the filed class in its DISCHARGE direction: sgirekills' assess stage reads `ok 9/9` through two "never answered" notes that also release its hold.

# Records check of Argon's late reading of the `fableheldform` design pass (fhfp1)

**What was compared.** Argon read only the design-pass packet (`argon_bundle_1.txt`, `argon_bundle_2.txt`, built before the
commit). Each item below is judged against what `6c2840d7` and HEAD actually do. HEAD was `5dbc000e` when the work began;
`1a59a47e` landed during it and changes only `fbctrlcmdkill.commits` in the ledger, so every finding holds at both, and
`edits.diff` applies to both (`git -c core.autocrlf=false apply --check`; CHANGELOG hunks land at offset +4 on `1a59a47e`).
None of the rows `6c2840d7` touched changed after it: a ledger diff `6c2840d7` -> `5dbc000e` touches only other rows.

Everything here is read-only on the repository: `git --no-optional-locks show/cat-file`, and Python over **copies** of the
ledger in `work\`. The receipts are listed at the end. `ST` below means `tools/pipeline/stage_tokens.py` at HEAD, `CSP` means
`tools/pipeline/check_stage_panels.py`, `CFQ` means `tools/pipeline/check_fable_queue.py`, and `DP1` means
`regress/artifacts/fableheldform/design_20261018/`.

