25238:   "state_note": "*** DO NOT CLOSE THIS ROW ON THE EXISTENCE OF `state_semantics`.  I ALMOST DID, 2026-08-29. ***\n\nThe block is written, all five observed states are documented (`closed`/`held`/`open`/\n`in work`/`dropped` -- census run 2026-08-29, 270 rows, none undocumented), and `_ADOPTED`\nsays the owner ratified it on 2026-08-25 with both amendments.  **That is the SEMANTICS half\nand it IS discharged.**  It is also the visible half, which is why it reads like the whole row.\n\n**The row's own `hold` names the other half: ~50 mis-stated instances across five classes,\nand it is ITS OWN RECORDS-CORRECTION ROUND** -- which additionally owes the standing second\nreview pass, because corrections repeat the errors they correct.  None of that is done.\n\nSo the state is right and the row is easy to misread as finished.  Recorded here because the\ncheapest defence against a wrong closure is a sentence at the place the closure gets made.\n\nPASS-1 PANEL: `_scratchpad/panel_20260825_030653_matrix`. SIX SEATS FIRED, FIVE ANSWERED, ONE DOWN. grok, codex, minimax-m3, kimi-k3:cloud and glm-5.2 answered; **agy FAILED on a SIXTH seat-failure class -- its own streaming transport dropped mid-answer** (`connection to the agent was interrupted ... subscriber fell behind updates, stalled for 6s`) -- and was re-fired, since a dropped connection is worth retrying where a wall is not. *** grok's verdict is the headline: DO NOT TRUST STATES. *** THE FINDINGS, by class. (1) STATE CONTRADICTS SUBSTANCE: `pitlatch` and `pitclobber` sit `in work` while every closure note says CLOSE and the residual their hold names is already discharged by a CLOSED carrier; `hpcshcpu` is `open` while its own note says DROP FROM THE ACTIONABLE QUEUE; `m8invpred` is `held` behind a codex wall discharged 2026-08-20; `reprowitness` is `in work` although the witness ladder it produced has been in the carrier since 2026-08-19; `gdromdmawidth` is `open` while admitting shipped code carries a comment under its name. (2) *** THE BURIAL SHAPE, TEXTBOOK FORM: `b115` and `b126` EACH CARRY TWO INDEPENDENT DEFECTS UNDER ONE ID. *** Closing either on one defect buries the other -- the exact hazard the carrier records. This is the highest-severity finding here. (3) STALE BLOCKERS: `gdromdmawidth` waits on `sh4gdromexit`, a carrier that will never do the work attributed to it. (4) UNEVIDENCED CLOSES: `rtcdet`, `gdromdmadefault`, `witnessinvisible` close asserting shipped substance with no commits; `b75` is a contested close with no recorded adjudication; `fbwitness` closes with an absent witness while its title claims a probe. (5) A CORRECTION THAT DID NOT PROPAGATE: a measured 4x inflation was corrected in `arcbiosubsan` and never in `sgiarcbiosoob`, which still asserts it -- mechanical rule 2 (grep for a claim's SIBLINGS before calling it fixed) failing in the ledger itself. *** AND A WARNING FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS ROUND, because the round that filed it got this wrong THREE TIMES: THE LEDGER HAS MULTIPLE SCHEMA ERAS AND A CHECKER THAT KNOWS ONE ERA INVENTS ANOMALIES. *** (a) Narrative lives in `entries` on 198 older rows and `state_note` on 19 newer ones; counting only the latter reported 191 rows with no narrative when the true number is ZERO. (b) `witness` is FORWARD-ONLY from 2026-08-19 (`CLAUDE.md:1191`), so of 16 closed-without-witness rows FOURTEEN are compliant and only `rtcdet` and `fbwitness` are owed. (c) `closure.by` postdates part of the ledger. (d) 'open/held WITH commits' looked like 16 anomalies and is ZERO -- all sixteen carry a HOLD marker, which is the normal batching state by design. **Establish the validity rule for each class BEFORE touching a cell, and print a denominator beside every count.**  *** THE SCOPE WAS MEASURED AND IT IS 12 ROWS, NOT ~50. The ~50 figure this row was FILED with was my own, and it was wrong for the FIFTH time in one session, by the same mechanism every previous time. *** Establishing each class's validity rule BEFORE counting -- which is what the adjudication demanded -- collapses the raw counts hard: A closed-without-witness 16 raw -> **2** (14 predate the 2026-08-19 forward-only cutoff and are COMPLIANT); B closed-without-commits 6 raw -> **3** (three are withdrawn / not-a-bug / documented-only); C open-or-held-with-commits 17 raw -> **1** (fifteen carry a HOLD, the normal batching state, and one was `asicbadaddr` itself, closed when #463 committed); D closed-without-closure.by 2 raw -> **2**; E state-contradicts-closure 30 raw -> **5**. *** RULE 5, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT COST THE MOST: `closure.note` MAY DISPOSE OF A STAGE, NOT THE ROW. *** The ledger defines four `phase_labels` -- assess, research, review, regress -- so `CLOSE (assess)` closes the ASSESS STAGE and the row legitimately stays `held`. TWENTY-FIVE of class E's thirty are that shape; `exitsweep`'s own hold says so out loud (*\"CLOSURE REVERTED ... the fable5 batch answered this row's held stage\"*). Reading a stage-scoped disposition as a row-scoped one manufactured 25 false defects. THE TWELVE THAT SURVIVE: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmadefault, witnessinvisible, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m437ord, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, cmtattr, hpcshcpu -- plus this row. Note the convergence worth trusting: `fbwitness`, `rtcdet`, `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` were named INDEPENDENTLY by the reading seats AND fall out of the mechanical census, which is the only kind of agreement that counts here.  RECONCILIATION, demanded by the adjudicator before either number may be quoted: the class column sums to **13 instances** and the repair set is **11 rows**. 13 - 1 dedup (`fbwitness` is in BOTH closed-no-witness and closed-no-commits) - 1 self (`ledgerstatelies` is this tracking row) = 11. Both numbers are correct; the arithmetic was simply never shown, which made the correction READ as contradicting its own column -- **a sixth counting defect sitting inside the correction that reports the fifth.**  *** THE ROUND'S CENTRAL FINDING, MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT WHAT EITHER SEAT CLAIMED: `state` IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THE RECORD. *** Two file-reading seats disagreed on `fbwitness` -- one said `CLOSE (assess)` disposes of a STAGE so the row must be `held`, the other said it retires the assessment so `closed` is fine. Settled empirically rather than by vote, and BOTH inferences are unsupported: **23 rows carry the IDENTICAL `closure.note` of `CLOSE (assess)` and they are spread across THREE states -- 12 `held`, 7 `in work`, 4 `closed`** (the closed four being ns16550, fablequeue, carrier, fbwitness). The obvious second hypothesis was tested and also fails: the number of STAGES with recorded entries is statistically identical across the three groups (mean 1.8 / 1.8 / 1.9, same min and max), so stage coverage does not explain the split either. **`state` and `closure.note` are independent fields with no enforced relationship**, so a reader cannot derive one from the other and neither can a checker. That is why 'DO NOT TRUST STATES' is the correct verdict, and it reframes this round: **the repair cannot be a mechanical rewrite of 11 cells, because there is no rule to rewrite them TO.** The round must first establish what `state` MEANS -- most plausibly 'is there work outstanding on this row', which is a different question from 'has any stage been adjudicated' -- and only then repair. ALSO REFUTED HERE, and worth keeping because it was checkable in two minutes: the claim that `fbwitness` is ALONE among its 2026-08-20 cohort in being closed. That cohort is 84 rows -- 17 closed, 43 held, 11 dropped, 13 in work -- and four of the closed share `fbwitness`'s exact closure string. A seat's argument from 'this row is the only one' is worth checking every time; this one was wrong.  *** FIVE SEATS, ELEVEN ROWS, AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS THE EVIDENCE RATHER THAN THE NOISE. *** agy, kimi-k3, glm-5.2, minimax-m3 and grok each assigned a correct state to all eleven rows, reading the same ledger under the same four validity rules stated in the brief. **They agree on only 4 of 11.** `hpcshcpu` drew THREE different answers (closed / dropped / held); six more drew two. This is what a field with no derivable meaning looks like from the outside: competent independent readers do not converge, because there is nothing to converge ON. *** IT ALSO PARTITIONS THE ROUND CLEANLY, WHICH IS THE USEFUL PART. *** **UNANIMOUS (4) -- and all four are ALREADY IN THE RIGHT STATE**: `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` (closed, missing `commits`), `m437ord` (closed, missing `closure.by`), `cmtattr` (held, correct as-is). What these need is FIELDS, not state changes, and a field that is absent is absent under any definition of `state` -- so they are repairable NOW, independently of the rule question. **SPLIT (7)**: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, hpcshcpu. Every one of these turns on what `state` MEANS, and none can be repaired until that is settled -- editing them now would be manufacturing the appearance of correctness by picking whichever seat I found most persuasive. **THE SPLIT IS ALSO A CHECK ON THE 23-ROW FINDING**, arrived at from the opposite direction: that one measured one closure string against three states; this one measures five readers against one row set. Both say the same thing and neither depends on the other's method.  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25, AND IT REFRAMED THE ROUND WHILE CONVICTING ME OF ONE OVERREACH. *** The ruling: `state` and `closure.note` are different TENSES. The note is a PAST-TENSE event log of what happened to a STAGE; `state` is a PRESENT-TENSE assertion of what the ROW still OWES. **Under that reading the multi-state spread of one closure string is the ledger WORKING AS DESIGNED, not the defect** -- rows that experienced the same stage event legitimately differ in what else they owe -- and the stage-coverage test failing is CORROBORATION rather than a second mystery, because state depends on what REMAINS, not on how much happened. **SO THE THING TO FILE IS THE MISSING SCHEMA SEMANTICS, NOT THE SPREAD.** That single absence is the cause behind six mis-counts in one session, two seats inferring opposite conventions from one string, and five seats agreeing on only 4 of 11 rows. The fix is written: a `state_semantics` block now sits in the ledger's own schema, PENDING OWNER RATIFICATION, forward-only in the same way `witness` was. *** MY OVERREACH, RETRACTED IN PLACE: I wrote that 'for most of the 11 there is no fact of the matter to restore -- only a decision'. THAT IS FALSE AND IT WAS CONVENIENT FOR ME*** -- it converts eleven tedious substance reads into one interesting rule question. What died was a repair METHOD (derive state from the note), not the fact of the matter, which is read from commits, gates, holds and filed questions -- exactly how `asicbadaddr` was closed, with no note convention needed. **The seminar is over in one paragraph; the eleven reads remain owed.** *** AND NO NUMBER IN THIS ROW MAY BE QUOTED AS A PREMISE UNTIL A SECOND PARTY CHECKS IT. *** I have mis-counted this ledger six times in one session. Every ruling above depends on the EXISTENCE of the spread, not its size -- ONE held row and ONE closed row sharing a closure string already prove non-derivability -- so the whole second-party check is two greps. The figures 23, 84 and the 1.8/1.8/1.9 stage means are UNVERIFIED SUPPORTING COLOUR, labelled as such, and are leads rather than premises.  *** A SEVENTH COUNTING HAZARD, FOUND BY MAKING IT: SEAT NAMES ARE NOT SEAT ANSWERS. *** While closing #463's stages I reported research at 9/9; it was 8/9. The `codex` entry on that stage is a **`[HELD AWAITING SEAT:` MARKER** -- a record that the seat is OUTSTANDING -- and I counted it because I counted seat NAMES. `check_stage_panels.py` excludes marker entries deliberately, and its own comment says exactly why: *'otherwise marking a gap would close it.'* **Writing down that a seat is missing would, under a name-count, make the stage look complete** -- the seat-failure-as-agreement shape in its purest mechanical form. **THE LEDGER CARRIES 44 SUCH MARKER ENTRIES**, each one a seat that has NOT answered. Any future census of panel coverage that counts names rather than answers over-reports by up to that much. Three marker tokens exist and all three must be excluded: `[HELD AWAITING SEAT:`, `[NOT A PANEL STAGE: SINGLE-SEAT RE-READ]`, and `[RUN UNDER THE PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY]`. *This belongs with the schema-semantics finding rather than beside it: `entries` is not a list of answers, it is a list of EVENTS, and some events record an absence. That is the same present-tense/past-tense confusion that `state` and `closure.note` embody, one level down.*  *** A NEW INSTANCE OF THIS ROW'S OWN DEFECT, FOUND 2026-08-25 BY THE ADJUDICATOR TRIPPING OVER IT WHILE ADJUDICATING THIS VERY BLOCK. *** Asked to rule on the queue, the fable5 seat reported that `idesync` \"is already closed as document-do-not-fix\". The ledger says `state = \"in work\"` with `commits` EMPTY. Both readings are half right, and the shape is new: *** THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BUT THE DOCUMENT HAS NOT SHIPPED. *** The row carries an opus5 REVIEW verdict (2026-08-20) of DOCUMENT-DO-NOT-FIX on a deciding fact nobody else raised -- the drive never advertises FLUSH CACHE, so the highest IDENTIFY word written never sets it -- plus a closed assess stage (`closure.by = fable5`, `CLOSE (assess)`). A SUBSTANCE reader therefore sees a finished decision and says \"closed\"; a STATE reader sees no commits and says \"unfinished\". Under the semantics now written, `closed` means discharged BY WORK, and a documenting commit IS work that has not happened -- so the state field is RIGHT and the adjudicator was WRONG, while the adjudicator was right about the only thing anyone would act on. *** THE LESSON IS THAT `document-do-not-fix` NEEDS ITS OWN DISCHARGE, AND NOTHING TRACKS IT. *** A row in this state is invisible to both readings: it looks decided to anyone reading the verdict and claimed to anyone reading the state, so nobody ships the document. That is an UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION with no carrier -- the class section I of precommit exists for, one field over. Candidate fix, NOT applied pending ratification: `closed` requires either a commit or an explicit `documented-only` closure badge, so an assessed-not-changed record cannot close without the record existing. The project's ethos already demands honest 'assessed, not changed' records; what is missing is anything that NOTICES when one was promised and never written.\n\n*** THE HUNT FOR A CONSUMED DECISION IS NO LONGER EMPTY.  2026-09-08, THREE FRESH INSTANCES, ONE OF\nWHICH COST REAL WORK. ***\n\nThis row was filed noting that mis-stated states had cost audit expense and reader distrust but\n**zero decisions**.  That is no longer true, and the instances are recorded here rather than in the\nrounds that found them, so the census stays in one place.\n\n**CLASS 1 (STATE CONTRADICTS SUBSTANCE) -- and this one CONSUMED A DECISION.**\n`sgirergbroundtrip` sat `open`, `commits: None`, `witness: None` while **`#505` had already fixed\nit** -- the tree carries the corrected GET arm AND a `#505:` provenance comment above it, and\n`CHANGELOG.md` carries a full round block.  I read the row's title at face value while scoping the\n`sgirandomcolour` round in the same file and **began designing around a defect that was already\ngone.**  Caught only by opening the source for an unrelated reason.  *Closed 2026-09-08 with its\ncommit and witness.*\n\n**A NEIGHBOURING CLASS THIS ROW DOES NOT YET NAME: MISSING COMMIT ATTRIBUTION ON A MULTI-ROUND\nCOMMIT.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508: four test-first rounds\"* and **not one of the\nfour rows carried its commit**:\n\n    sgirergbroundtrip  #505   state stale AND commits None AND witness None   <- worst\n    lcaconfwidth       #506   commits None\n    alphaswpctxptbr    #507   commits None\n    alphapfnoffset     #508   commits None\n\nEach verified before correcting: the commit's diff touches the right source file and carries the\nmatching round tag (`#506` x6 in `dev_lca.c`, `#507` x6 in `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`, `#508` x4 in\n`memory_alpha.c`).  All three attributions restored; **states deliberately left alone**, since\n`in work` may be correct for a row with residual obligations and changing it would be a claim I had\nnot checked.\n\n> **THE MECHANISM, and it is why this class had not appeared before: a commit shipping SEVERAL\n> rounds is ONE act to the committer and N rows to the ledger, so the per-row field is what falls\n> out.  A single-round commit cannot fail this way.**  That is a different generator from the five\n> classes already enumerated here, and it predicts the failure recurs at exactly the next\n> multi-round commit.\n\n**HOW THEY WERE FOUND, in order, because the order is the lesson:** the state gap BY ACCIDENT\n(opening source while scoping something else); then the three attribution gaps by asking the general\nquestion -- *\"which non-closed rows are named by a CHANGELOG round-block heading?\"* -- which is\nprecise because the heading format is fixed (`# \\`#NNN\\` <rowid>: <title>`).  **That query returned\n7 candidates of which 4 were legitimate `in work` rows**, so it is a candidate list needing\nrow-by-row verification and NOT a population claim.  *This row's own history is a warning against\nquoting a count here, so none is quoted.*",
25239:   "hold_question": "The SEMANTICS half is discharged (state_semantics written, owner-ratified 2026-08-25, all five observed states documented against a 270-row census).  The other half -- ~50 mis-stated instances across five classes -- is NOT, and it is its own records-correction round.  THE QUESTION IS THREE-PART.  (1) SCOPE: is a ~50-instance sweep one round, or is it per-class?  It additionally owes the standing SECOND review pass because corrections repeat the errors they correct, and this project has three recorded instances of exactly that.  (2) PRIORITY: does it earn a round at all, given that the hunt for a stale state CONSUMING a decision came back empty when this row was filed -- and that the hunt is now NON-EMPTY (see the 2026-09-08 instances below: a stale `open` sent me designing a round around an already-fixed defect).  Does one consumed decision flip it from documented-only to actionable, or is the bar higher?  (3) THE BURIAL SHAPE: `b115` and `b126` each carry TWO independent defects under ONE id, so closing either on one defect buries the other.  grok called this the highest-severity finding in the pass-1 panel.  Should splitting those two ids be pulled OUT of the sweep and done first as its own small round, since a burial is a permanent loss while a wrong state is a recoverable one?",
25240-   "note": "*** 2026-09-11: FILING A ROW WITHOUT ITS BUG-FILE ENTRY REDDENED THE GATE THREE TIMES TODAY -- ALL THREE MINE, ALL THREE THE SAME MINUTE-LATER FIX. ***\n\n`codexfarmclobber`, then `landiskintc` + `landiskboardreg`, then `agentmodelwall`.  Each time: file the ledger row, run the gate, meet `BUGFILE_SYNC_FAIL`, write the entry, green again.\n\n**The gate is working exactly as designed** -- it exists because *an OPEN row nobody can find by reading `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` is work a fresh session will not see* -- and it caught all three before any commit.  **So this is not a gate defect and not a records defect; it is a sequencing habit.**\n\n> **A NEW ROW IS TWO WRITES, NOT ONE.**  The ledger row and the bug-file entry are the same act and belong in the same script.  By the third occurrence I was predicting the red before running the check -- which is the tell that it had become a ritual rather than a lesson.\n\n*Deliberately NOT mechanised beyond what exists: the gate already refuses the commit, which is the enforceable half.  Adding a helper that writes both would be reasonable and is an owner call, not a round's -- and this project has measured that a check policing the minority of a class while alarming on prose gets switched off.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 SECOND-PARTY CHECK -- **THIS AUDIT IS THAT CHECK FOR 8 OF THE ELEVEN.  Folded in here rather than filed as a new row, because this row's own text asks for exactly this: 'the eleven reads remain owed ... pending a second party checking each one.'** ***\n\n**METHOD AND DENOMINATOR:** a subagent was given eight rows and asked to compare each row's `state` field against its actual SUBSTANCE -- what the row's body, commits and closure say happened -- rather than against its headline.  **8 of 11 checked; 3 remain owed** (`witnesssilent`, `queueunrated`, `seatunfinished` are NOT covered by this pass and their reads are still outstanding).\n\n=== THE FINDINGS, THREE DISPOSITIONS ===\n\n**CLOSEABLE NOW (3):**\n  * `stdbufasan` -- substance supports closure as it stands.\n  * `nulheredoc` -- **closeable ONCE ITS COMMIT IS NAMED.  It was missing, and is added by this pass: `25da404`, verified by `git log` before writing (*'precommit Y: guard tracked text files against NUL bytes'* -- the commit subject matches the row's subject exactly).**  A row that shipped a guard and names no commit is the state-vs-substance defect in its purest form: the record says nothing landed and something did.\n  * `hpcshcpu` -- closeable, but **as `dropped`, NOT `closed`**.  *This is the `es438` shape this project already recorded: a state that misdescribes the work.  There the error ran the other way -- `dropped` stamped on shipped, witnessed, reviewed work, which a future reader trusting states reads as 'not done'.*  **State change deliberately NOT applied in this pass**, because a bug-file sync audit is in flight against the same file and two writers to one record is how this project produced `BUGFILE_SYNC_FAIL` three times.  *A new row is two writes, not one* -- and so is a closure.\n\n**CLOSEABLE ONCE X (3):** `smdatefmt` (commits `6e36943`, verified), `idesync`, `pitclobber` -- each has a named precondition rather than an open question.\n\n**NOT CLOSEABLE (2):**\n  * `smnotland` -- substance does not support closure.\n  * `wdcoracle` -- **and this one is worth its own sentence: the quoted phrase the row relies on sits DIRECTLY BELOW ITS OWN REFUTATION in the same field.**  A reader who greps for the phrase lands on the claim; a reader who reads the paragraph lands on the refutation.  *That is the stale-text-grep collision this project already records -- a naive search cannot tell an ASSERTION from a QUOTATION OF ONE -- appearing inside a row rather than inside the carrier.*\n\n=== CROSS-CUTTING, AND THE THIRD ITEM IS A LIVE CHECK-THAT-CANNOT-FAIL ===\n  * **0 of 8 carry live HELD markers** -- so none of these is waiting on a seat, and the held-marker machinery is not what is holding them.\n  * **5 of 5 cited commit hashes are REAL**, re-verified here by `git log --oneline -1` on each (`25da404`, `020a672`, `6e36943`, `0922465`) rather than taken from the audit.\n  * *** `pitclobber`'s witness reads `none needed -- the defect was fixed by #439 (020a672), which also shipped regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py (308 lines) as its detector.`  **That string MECHANICALLY PASSES `check_witness.py`'s `none` regex while being doctrinally wrong: it describes, in its own words, exactly what the `detector:<path>` term was adopted on 2026-08-27 to express.** ***  The checker cannot fail on it -- the field opens with the one token the regex accepts, and everything after it is prose the regex never reads.  *So the conflation `detector:` was adopted to END is still standing in at least this row, and the term's arrival did not migrate the rows that motivated it.*\n\n**A WIDER SWEEP WAS RUN ON THAT LAST POINT RATHER THAN LEFT AS A SUGGESTION.**  Across all 461 rows, **164 carry a `witness` beginning `none`; of those, 16 ALSO NAME A COMMIT** -- `a1a2`, `asiceventaddr`, `carrier`, `census`, `constblind`, `fablequeue`, `gdromdmawidth`, `m8batc`, `mrwstore2`, `ns16550`, `ovsync`, `panelseatlabel`, `pcc2genctlc040alias`, `pitclobber`, `probewiring`, `wdccdbmidxfer`.  **Most are legitimately `none`** (records-only rounds, policy rows, and honest 'none yet' fields).  *An adversarial audit of all sixteen is in flight; the disposition proposed to it is FORWARD-ONLY -- only rows still `open`/`in work`/`held` are actionable, because a rule that retro-fails a large population is a rule that gets switched off, which is worse than a warning.*\n\n*Provenance: the check was run by a SONNET subagent while both Anthropic seats were walled -- see [[agentmodelwall]].  It is a READING, and it is scored as one.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ID-IN-SOURCE SWEEP, ADJUDICATED (Opus 5, read-only).  **A = 0, B = 19, C = 3 -- THE SWEEP'S ONE REAL HIT WAS THE ONE I HAD ALREADY VERIFIED BY HAND.** ***\n\n**THE METHOD.**  Live rows (`open`/`in work`/`held`) with an EMPTY `commits` field whose id appears in COMMITTED `src/` -- **23 of 284**.  The hypothesis was that some are fixes that shipped without the row being updated, as [[m88kexcdeliver]] was.\n\n**THE RESULT: of the 22 sent for classification, ZERO are shipped fixes.**  19 are **(B) the round's own disclosure of the defect it declined to fix**, 3 are **(C) the row appearing as a third party in a comment about something else**.  *No `commits:` is owed by any of them and no state should change.*\n\n=== THE DISCRIMINATOR, AND IT IS MECHANICAL ===\n**`m88kexcdeliver` has a shape none of the 22 share: SEVEN tag-on-CODE-LINE hits** -- `implemented = true;  /*  m88kexcdeliver  */`.  Across the 22, **30 unique committed hit lines (31 grep results; `ppcrldimimask` and `ppcrlwimiwidth` SHARE line 2493), and 0 of 30 contain an assignment, guard, constant or branch.**  Every one is prose inside a `/* */`.\n\nEvery B carries an explicit NON-FIX verb, which is what makes the class readable at a glance: *filed / tracked / recorded / not taken here / not reopened here / not this round's / still lands here by design / STILL halt / still not implemented / stays filed / standing do-not-code ruling*.  **These are the ledger working as intended -- a round closing its scope and naming what it left.**\n\n*** THE WORKTREE-VS-HEAD CAUTION WAS NOT HYPOTHETICAL AND IT WOULD HAVE COST TWO LIVE ROWS. ***\nThe brief required `git grep ... HEAD` rather than a worktree grep.  Measured difference:\n\n| file | row | HEAD hits | worktree hits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `dev_lca.c` | `lcabadval` | 1 | **2** -- the extra is my in-flight `#518` tag |\n| `dev_sgi_re.c` | `sgirandomcolour` | 1 | **3** -- two extra `#519` tags |\n| `cpu_ppc_instr.c` | both PPC rows | 1 each | 1 each |\n\n**`#518` and `#519` appear ZERO times in `src/` at HEAD.**  *Had the sweep read the worktree, `lcabadval` (HIGH) and `sgirandomcolour` (HIGH) would BOTH have classified as shipped fixes -- marking two live rows done on the strength of my own uncommitted edits.*  That is the measure-your-own-in-flight-work-as-evidence trap, and the only thing that stopped it was naming the tree.\n\n=== THE HONEST BOUND ON MY OWN METHOD, which is the part worth keeping ===\n> **An id-in-source sweep finds only fixes that TAG THE ROW ID.  A shipped fix tagged only `/* #NNN */` is INVISIBLE to it.**  So *23 of 284* is a **LOWER BOUND** on shipped-but-unrecorded, the yield is **1 real in 23 hits (~4%)**, and **the actual staleness risk sits in the 261 rows with no id in source, where this method reports nothing either way.**\n\n*That is the denominator rule turned on a sweep of mine: `0 of 0` and `0 of 41` are different claims, and so are `1 of 23 checked` and `1 of 284`.  The sweep is worth running and is not worth trusting as coverage.*\n\n**THREE SUB-SHAPES OF B, recorded because they look like staleness and are not:**\n  * **residual-of-a-shipped-fix** (`m88kexcsfu1`, `m88kexctaildefault`, `wdcconsumedattempts`, `pcc2pscaleflood`) -- a fix shipped nearby and the row IS the uncovered remainder, said so in the same comment;\n  * **held-shut-by-a-shipped-guard** ([[sgilinearoob]]) -- `#497`'s `len != 8` guard mitigates the OOB without fixing it, and the comment says the fix *'deliberately does NOT add a clamp'* because the row is filed to fix all five arms with ONE design.  *My hypothesis about this row was right and my count was wrong: it is **6 lines across two MIRRORED arms**, not one.*\n  * **made-reachable-by-a-shipped-fix** ([[m88kdslotshadow]]) -- `#502` turned a latent upstream arm into an ordinary-fault path, so the row is open BECAUSE of the fix.\n\n*** AND A PRIOR INSTANCE OF THE CLASS I WAS SWEEPING FOR WAS FOUND ALREADY SELF-CORRECTED: *** `m88kexctaildefault`'s own note records that its `cpu_m88k.c:796` citation **predates the row** -- *'This row was CITED by #502's product comment and CHANGELOG as if it existed before it did ... filed 2026-09-06 to make the citation true.'*  *A round citing a row id that does not yet exist is the same records defect from the other end, and that one was caught and fixed by its own author.*\n\n**Corroboration for work in flight:** the classification independently confirmed both PowerPC defects are STILL LIVE AT HEAD rather than trusting their rows -- `X(rldimi)` still has `while (sh-- != 0)` feeding `while (m != 63 - sh)`, and `X(rlwimi)` still rotates a `MODE_uint_t` with no `uint32_t` truncation.  *Those are the two the uncommitted diff repairs, and a pass 2 on it is in flight.*"
