42523-   "state_note": "`check_bugfile_sync.py` verifies that every OPEN LEDGER ROW is findable in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.  It does NOT verify the reverse, and its docstring says why:\n\n    \"The reverse direction is already clean (no OUTSTANDING_BUGS header lacks a ledger\n     row), which is what the es438 fix bought.  This closes the other direction.\"\n\n*** THAT SENTENCE IS NOW FALSE.  Measured 2026-09-07: 190 headings, 398 ledger rows, and **33 headings with no ledger row** -- TEN of them filed by me tonight. ***\n\n> This is the carrier's own **absence-record-is-time-indexed** class, on a checker rather than on a mutant: *an absence record must NAME ITS WARRANT, and any edit touching that warrant RE-RUNS THE DEATH MEASUREMENT.*  The warrant is named -- honestly and in the right place -- and **nothing re-runs it**, so the check's scope silently stopped matching its justification.\n\n**WHY IT MATTERS AND IS NOT SYMMETRY FOR ITS OWN SAKE.**  The checker's own reasoning applies unchanged in this direction: *\"ledger.json is the pipeline's source of truth -- per-seat, per-phase, machine-read by gen_dashboard.py and check_stage_panels.py.  A round with no ledger row is invisible.\"*  A row filed only in OUTSTANDING_BUGS gets no dashboard cell, no stage tracking, and no fable-queue visibility.  **`armbfxwidth32` -- an active round with a measured witness -- was in exactly that state until this row was written.**\n\n**SCOPE IF IT IS BUILT:** the same shape as the existing check -- open items only, forward-only from a dated cutoff, so the 23 PRE-EXISTING headings are reported and not failed.  A rule that retro-fails 23 rows blocks the next commit on reconstruction nobody scheduled, and *a rule that does that gets switched off*.\n\n*Not built here: it changes what every commit must satisfy, and owes two negative controls -- red on a heading with no row, green once the row exists.*  *** COUNT RE-DERIVED 2026-09-17: **23 of 274**, not the 33 this row's warrant carries from 2026-09-07.  The class is LIVE and has SHRUNK. ***  THE DERIVER, so the next reader runs it rather than trusting this number -- which is the rule this project has had to re-learn five times on one marker census:\n    heads = re.findall(r'^#{2,4}\\s+`([a-zA-Z0-9_]+)`', OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md, re.M)\n    ids   = {r['id'] for r in ledger['rows']}\n    missing = [h for h in sorted(set(heads)) if h not in ids]\nMeasured against the WORKING TREE (which carries this session's uncommitted rows), not HEAD -- and that distinction matters here, because six of today's filings would otherwise show as missing. **THE 23:** `asicdefaultflood`, `checkcunwired`, `clausedbound`, `dashphasedrop`, `detectorescapes`, `gdromoob`, `gdromxfrflood`, `imagesmd`, `lcastubparity`, `sh4a4gate`, `sh4dmacintr`, `sh4fmtcast`, `sh4pclockstatic`, `sh4sqexpevt`, `shsweep`, `tablebasenotfull`, `wdcaltctrlcov`, `wdcaltdecode`, `wdcatapisig`, `wdccylstale`, `wdcletwins`, `wdcponsig`, `wdcresetirq`. *** WHY THIS IS THE INVERSE OF A GATE THAT ALREADY EXISTS, AND WHY THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE WHOLE ROW: `precommit_check.sh` section P fails when a LEDGER row has no bug-file entry -- it caught me THREE times today.  **NOTHING checks the other direction**, so a heading can sit in the human queue for months with no ledger row, invisible to every gate. ***  **CORROBORATION FROM AN INDEPENDENT SWEEP THE SAME DAY.**  A pattern-keyed audit (shell denied, so grep-only) sampled 25 such headings and found 23 not in the ledger -- converging on the same number from a different method.  It also usefully REJECTED two tempting generators as false positives: **`owes a row` is DETECTOR-row language**, not queue language, and **`FILE -` is overwhelmingly `FILE-READING`** or `THE DIFF FILE`.  It separately found **3 CHANGELOG `filed as \\`id\\`` names that are not ledger ids**, and one pair (TEA / `EXPEVT_RESET_POWER`) named in a FILED-not-fixed block with **no id at all** -- which cannot be caught by any id-matching sweep and needs a human read. **DISPOSITION: the count is corrected and the class is NOT swept here.**  23 headings is a reconstruction job nobody scheduled, and this project's own rule is that a check which retro-fails a backlog blocks the next commit and therefore gets switched off.  If a checker is ever built it takes the dated-cutoff shape section P already uses -- forward-only, older entries reported and not failed.",
42524:   "note": "*** 2026-09-11 AUDIT (Opus 5, read-only).  **AND A SIXTH STATE VALUE NOW EXISTS THAT IS IN NEITHER ENUMERATION, so one row was invisible to the gate in BOTH directions -- and the file's own header predicted exactly this.** ***\n\n**RE-MEASURED, with the deriver rather than the stored number.**  `check_bugfile_sync.py`'s `unsynced()` (`:86-99`) was re-implemented byte-faithfully against the current pair of files: **322 open-state rows, 13 with no bug-file entry, 0 of them newer than `CUTOFF = 2026-08-20`** -- so the gate reports PASS, and the 10 legacy ones are forgiven by design.  *(The 3 new ones filed this morning were given entries in the same pass; a new row is two writes, not one.)*\n\n=== THE FOUR GAPS, IN DESCENDING COST ===\n\n**1.  THE CONVERSE IS NOT CHECKED AT ALL** -- this row's own subject, re-confirmed.  `:24-27` says it is deliberate, because demanding an entry for a closed row *'would fight the file's design'*.  **But the file's charter says *'Only genuinely OPEN items live here.  Resolved ones are removed, not annotated'* -- and NOTHING ENFORCES THE REMOVAL HALF.**  Measured today: of 72 id-bearing entries in the OPEN LIST, **12 name rows that are `closed`.**\n\n**2.  *** `OPEN_STATES` IS SHORT BY ONE, AGAIN. ***  `:62` is `(\"held\", \"in work\", \"open\")`, and `:59-61` records that the list was *derived from the data*.  A sixth value now occurs: [[pcc2c040assertrow]] carried `state: \"fixed\"`, which is in `OPEN_STATES`, in the terminal set, and in `ledger.json`'s own `state_semantics` block -- **none of them.**  So it was invisible in both directions, and it has no bug-file entry, which nothing reported.  **The file's `:54-57` says *'when one enumeration is found short, GREP THE OTHERS'* -- this is that warning landing on the file that carries it.**  *A list derived from the data goes stale the moment the data grows a new value, and the value that broke it was added by this project three days earlier.*  **Normalised to `closed` in the same pass**, since the row's work is genuinely done (`9fa07fe`, a `detector:` witness, three clause-(d) mutants killed).\n\n**3.  THE 'REVERSE IS ALREADY CLEAN' CLAIM IS FALSE AT ENTRY LEVEL, NOT ONLY AT HEADING LEVEL.**  At HEADING level it still holds.  At ENTRY level it does not: `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:611` files **`shortthreshold` and there was no ledger row** -- the id appears in `ledger.json` only inside the prose note of the closed row [[shortread]].  ***That is the `es438`/`lunafuse` shape the checker's OWN HEADER records at `:13-15`*** -- *'filed in OUTSTANDING_BUGS and given no ledger row -- so the fix reproduced the defect one layer in'* -- **recurring, in the file written to prevent it, and the bug-file entry even names the class it is an instance of.**  Filed this morning as [[shortthreshold]].\n\n**4.  EXISTENCE, NOT CURRENCY.**  A match is `` `id` `` anywhere in ~19,000 lines, so an id mentioned only inside a 2026-08-14 retrospective satisfies the gate as fully as a live OPEN-LIST entry.\n\n*** AND ONE FINDING RUNS THE OPPOSITE WAY, WHICH IS WHY THE DIRECTION IS NAMED EVERY TIME: *** [[diskimagesign]] was `in work` in the LEDGER while the bug file already recorded the fix as landed and measured (`18893de`, *gate_mips PASS (was SKIP)*).  **The human-facing file was right and the machine source of truth was wrong.**  Normalised to `closed`.  *A reader who learned 'the bug file goes stale' from the other twelve findings would draw exactly the wrong conclusion from this one.*\n\n**REVERSE CHECK ON THE POPULATION THE GATE EXISTS FOR: 21 `sev: high` open-state rows examined, ZERO missing from the bug file.**  The gate's own direction is clean on its own population."
42525-  },
