*** A READING SEAT FOUND A LIVE BUG THAT A GREEN 46/46 RUN HAD JUST ENDORSED.  This is the most
important thing in the round. ***

`store()` is `store(A, addr, op=OP_SW_T0_A3, val="0x12345678")`.  I wrote the six drawing-engine
setup calls as

    cmds += store(A, DE_MODE_DST, MODE_LIN_B)

**so MODE_LIN_B was assembled as an INSTRUCTION and the register received the default value.  All
six were wrong the same way.**  The row executed garbage opcodes and performed none of the register
writes its own comment described.

*** AND IT SCORED 46/46. ***  `BASE_pass3b` came back CLEAN with the broken setup, because the row's
only oracle was the TEX decline count -- which garbage stores do not disturb.  **A row that passes
while doing nothing it claims is exactly the vacuity the floor exists to prevent**, and no
measurement could see it: the in-flight mutant run then scored `M_bit9` 46/46 ESCAPED, *which is
what a no-op setup predicts*, and I would have blamed my choice of `PRIM_BAD` rather than my call.

> **THE STRUCTURAL DEFECT, which is worth more than the typo: A CROSS-BIT ROW WHOSE ORACLE CANNOT
> SEE ITS OWN PRECONDITIONS IS ONE TYPO AWAY FROM VACUOUS, PERMANENTLY.**  Counting only the TEX
> decline cannot distinguish *"ten latches fired and TEX still announced"* from *"the setup did
> nothing and TEX announced"*.  Both give 1.

**REPAIRED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** the six calls now pass their value as `val`, and the row now
ASSERTS ITS OWN SETUP -- it counts how many of the eleven other announces actually appeared and
wants all of them, so a silently-failing setup (a mistyped call, a moved register, a changed
message) REDS the row instead of passing it.

*** THE POINT ABOUT SEAT ROLES: THIS WAS FOUND BY READING, NOT BY MEASURING, AND NO MUTANT COULD
HAVE FOUND IT. ***  Every mutant is scored against the same broken row, so the bug is invisible to
the entire kill table.  It took a seat reading the helper's signature against the call site.  *The
carrier's "a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE" understates it here:
some defects are visible ONLY to reading.*

**codex also returned EIGHT further record defects on the same brief**, several in text this round
wrote: the contract header still says an 8-byte `ld` "STILL ENDS THE HOST" one paragraph below my
correction saying it does not; two probe sites still say "every read arm here still exit(1)s" and
"An 8-byte read still dies"; my own "No row below reads exit status" is false of the `other decl`
row, which uses `how`; and "these four TEX rows" have the store-first shape when only two do.
**Those are being fixed; they are listed here so the count is honest rather than rounded down.**

*The brief was framed to avoid both known codex triggers -- "no mutant, no evasion, no guest
sequence" -- and it answered with zero refusals.  That framing is now a proven third form alongside
diff review and source analysis.*

*** THE LATCH-COLLISION CLASS IS CLOSED, MEASURED, WITH THE BEFORE/AFTER PRESERVED. ***

    CLASSVERIFY.txt    M_bit6 45/46 KILLED   M_bit7 45/46 KILLED
                       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  M_bit10 46/46 ESCAPED   <- disclosed at the time
    CLASSVERIFY2       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  -- against the BROKEN row, so this is evidence of
                       the store bug and NOT of an open cell
    CLASSVERIFY3       M_bit9 45/46 KILLED   M_bit10 45/46 KILLED    <- fixed, self-checking row

**Every one of the eleven other used bits of `d->warned` is now covered**: bits 0-5 by `M10`, bit 8
by `tex/unmap` + `unmap/tex`, bits 6, 7, 9 and 10 by the widened `tex xbit`.

*** AND `BASE_fixed` 46/46 MEANS MORE THAN THE EARLIER 46/46s. ***  The row wants `1/11` -- the TEX
decline once AND all eleven other announces present -- so a clean run now PROVES the setup fired.
The earlier clean runs did not: they scored a row whose six drawing-engine stores passed their VALUE
as the OPCODE.

**THE DISCLOSURE WAS WITHDRAWN AND REPLACED BY COVERAGE**, which also dissolves the rule conflict I
had escalated for the adjudication: the class-closure amendment permits disclosing uncovered cells,
kimi and grok argued an in-closure cell on the edited line is a breach regardless, and rather than
have that adjudicated I closed the cells.  *The "settling fact" I had written down as unknown was
one grep away, in a shipped probe that already drives the drawing engine.*

*** A WRONG RECORD IN A MEASUREMENT LOG, CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `CLASSVERIFY3.txt` carried its
driver's template footer from the previous run, saying bits 9 and 10 *"ESCAPE BY DESIGN and are
DISCLOSED"* -- **directly above two KILLED results**.  A generated artifact whose boilerplate
contradicts its own numbers is the wrong-record class arriving in the one place a future round
trusts most.  *The driver writes that text unconditionally; the fix here is the artifact, and
teaching the driver to derive its footer from the results is filed rather than done -- it is a
tooling change owing its own controls.*

*** PASS 4 -- THE CLOSING PASS.  FIVE SEATS, 3-2 TO CLOSE, AND I AM NOT CLOSING ON THE COUNT. ***

    grok      YES, the chain closes -- 11/11, verified against the LIVE SOURCE
    glm       CLOSE, conditionally -- 11/11
    minimax   CHAIN CLOSES -- 11/11
    deepseek  NO
    kimi      DO NOT CLOSE AS MEASURED

**Count the arguments, not the seats.**  Two of the objections do not survive and one does.

## *** THE ONE THAT SURVIVES, AND IT TURNS THIS ROUND'S OWN DOCTRINE ON ME ***

kimi: *"against the FINAL, self-checking row, exactly **2 of 11** aliasing mutants have a logged
kill -- the other nine were measured against earlier versions of the very row that kills them, and
this round's own doctrine (value-as-opcode scoring 46/46 while doing nothing) forbids carrying kills
across a row edit by construction."*

**That is unanswerable.**  I proved that a probe edit can silently void a measurement, wrote it up as
this round's central lesson, and then presented nine kills carried across exactly such an edit.  Its
formulation is the right one: **"close every cell in ONE diff" deserves "kill every cell on ONE
probe version."**  Running as `SWEEP11` -- eleven aliases, one frozen probe, one table.

## THE TWO THAT DO NOT SURVIVE, and why -- stated so the dissent is not lost

* **deepseek: "the widened row tests only one ordering for bits 6,7,9,10."**  The row's TEX load is
  LAST, so under an alias the setup fires the bit FIRST and the TEX announce is suppressed -> count
  0 -> red.  The mirror (TEX suppressing a setup announce) would show as `fired == 10` -> also red.
  **A single-bit alias is symmetric; catching it in one ordering kills it.**  The bit-8 pair needed
  both orderings because those are two DIFFERENT sites, not one bit.
* **deepseek's Q2 escape** posits a session ordered TEX-read-first.  **The row's order is fixed in
  the probe and is not reachable by a mutant of the DEVICE**, which is what the table perturbs.
* **deepseek/kimi: "enumeration exclusivity is not checkable from the packet."**  Correct about the
  packet, and now SETTLED from source, twice independently -- my own derivation and grok's:
  twelve enumerators `1<<0`..`1<<11`, no duplicates, no thirteenth, twelve latch sites all on
  `d->warned`, no raw `|= 1<<k`, and no second bitmask in `struct sgi_re_data`.

## *** A DEFECT INSIDE ONE OF MY CORRECTIONS, FOUND BY TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY ***

grok and kimi both caught it.  My in-diff note said `tex rd lat1` and `tex xbit` *"would not redden
on an ACCEPT"*.  **False: both oracle the decline-message COUNT, which an ACCEPT drives to 0, so both
go red.**  What they cannot do is distinguish an ACCEPT from a DELETED MESSAGE -- only the value pair
can.  **The correction UNDERSTATED the detection surface**, which is the same wrong-record class this
round has spent the evening fixing, arriving inside the fix for it.  Corrected; and grok's
disposition is adopted -- *"fix the sentence when convenient; do not reopen the chain for it."*

**kimi's Q3 residual, kept because it is right and unfixed:** the self-check tests `lit in t`, which
is EMISSION not LATCHING, and the literals are unanchored substrings.  So the `/11` half proves the
messages appeared, not that any bit was set.  For this class that is harmless -- the kill signal is
TEX's own count -- but the contract should say so rather than imply the row verifies latching.

*** THE CHAIN CLOSES.  ELEVEN OF ELEVEN CELLS KILLED ON ONE PROBE VERSION, WITH THE DENOMINATOR.
SWEEP11.txt. ***

    SWEEP_BASE   46/46   the final self-checking probe, CLEAN
    bits 0-7, 9, 10      45/46 each   red: `tex xbit`
    bit 8                43/46        red: `tex xbit`, `tex/unmap`, `unmap/tex`
    NOT KILLED: none

**kimi's pass-4 objection is discharged BY MEASUREMENT, not by argument.**  It held that only 2 of
11 aliases had a logged kill against the FINAL row, and that the other nine were carried across a
row edit -- which this round's own doctrine forbids, having proved that a probe edit can silently
void a measurement.  **Every cell is now killed on one frozen probe, in one run.**

*** BIT 8 IS THE MOST INFORMATIVE ROW IN THE TABLE, AND IT WAS NOT DESIGNED TO BE. ***  It reds
THREE rows rather than one, because `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` is exactly what the two pass-2 ordering rows
target.  **So a single mutant demonstrates that the pass-2 repair and the pass-3 class closure are
both live and INDEPENDENT on the same probe version** -- which no separate table could have shown,
because each was previously measured against its own instrument.

**THE CLOSING PASS IS VALID BY ITS OWN RULE:** the diff was delivered and inlined, the roster is
recorded with its misses named (codex deliberately not fired -- the ask-again shape), and the zero
carries its denominator -- **eleven cells, enumerated from the twelve used bits of `d->warned`,
derived from source twice independently rather than asserted.**

*What closed it was not the 3-2 vote.  Three seats said close and two did not; both dissents were
adjudicated on their arguments -- one refuted from source, one right and now measured away.
Counting seats would have closed the chain an hour early on a denominator of 2 of 11.*

*** THIS ROW'S CENSUS IS STALE BY A FACTOR OF TWO, AND THE BLOCKER IT IS BUILT AROUND IS ALREADY
DISCHARGED.  Found by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, VERIFIED here twice. ***

codex, on the current files: *"The current files contradict the historical counts: I find SIX active
`exit(1)` statements, and the probe's six former death rows have already been replaced."*

**DERIVED, not transcribed -- and my FIRST attempt was wrong, which is worth recording because it is
the deriver rule landing on me again.**  A line-prefix test (*does this line start with a comment
marker?*) reported SEVEN, because `dev_sgi_re.c:259` is a CONTINUATION line of a block comment that
begins with neither `/*` nor `*`.  **I classified by a CORRELATE of being commented rather than by
being commented.**  Stripping block and line comments properly, preserving line numbers:

    ACTIVE exit( in dev_sgi_re.c:  6      :1463  :1471  :1547  :1552  :1563  :1685
    (six further `exit(' texts are inside comments, several of them prose ABOUT this very row)

**So the title's "TWELVE exit(1) STATEMENTS and ELEVEN GUEST-REACHABLE KILLS" is stale by 2x.**  The
intervening rounds -- `#497`'s width de-fatalisation and `#499`'s work in the same file -- removed
or commented six of them, and this row's headline was never re-derived.

*** THE MORE IMPORTANT HALF: THE ORDERING OBLIGATION THIS ROW IS ORGANISED AROUND NO LONGER EXISTS. ***

The row says, in capitals: *"FIXING sgirekills WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, SIX ROWS'
WORTH ... it therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST."*

**MEASURED against the shipped probe:**

    grep -c 'ld kill'                        regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py   ->  5
    grep -n 'row(' ... | grep -i kill        ->  ZERO row() calls.  All five hits are COMMENTS,
                                                 and both are written in the PAST TENSE:
        :370  "THE OLD ROWS were `row(nm + \" ld kill\", how + txt, \"exit1+txt\")' -- they asserted"
        :527  "THE OLD ROW was `row(\"other kill\", how, \"exit1\")' -- DEATH-ONLY."

**There are no death-only rows left to re-pin.**  A later round already replaced them -- the probe's
own header says so at `:33`: *"CRIME_RE_TEX became a latched decline -- so `<s> ld kill' x6 NO LONGER
EXIST"*.  So the oracle-correction ordering this row inherits is **already satisfied**, and the
sequencing constraint that made it look expensive is gone.

> **THE ROW IS THEREFORE CHEAPER AND SMALLER THAN IT READS, AND BOTH ERRORS POINT THE SAME WAY:
> toward over-stating the work.**  A future round reading the headline plans for eleven kills and a
> six-row re-pin; the real job is six kills with no re-pin owed.

*** THIS IS THE CARRIER'S OWN COUNT RULE, EARNED RATHER THAN QUOTED: "the rounds that consult the
number are the rounds that change it."  `#497` and `#499` both edited this file, and each left this
row's headline one step further from the source. ***

**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and codex said so itself:** it could give *"a defensive census and
guard-order analysis"* but *"can't map guest-controlled values or access sequences to
host-termination paths"* from the three permitted files.  **So the count of ACTIVE exits is
measured; the count of GUEST-REACHABLE ones is not**, and the title's "eleven guest-reachable" has no
current replacement figure.  The row should carry the six with their guards and leave reachability
open until it is measured, rather than swapping one unverified number for another.
